  "image_name": "790ecd8976f1e534bc692a88a9ba414d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several common geometries for surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) and related near-field methods, all built around gold nanostructures interacting with target molecules. The left side shows the simplest case: dispersed Au nanoparticles in a colloidal solution, with small red dots representing analyte molecules around the particles. The rest compares more structured arrangements on surfaces.\n\nOne approach uses a rough solid substrate covered with closely packed metal nanoparticles, creating many contact regions where molecules can adsorb. A related “gap-mode” design places nanoparticles above a metal-like surface with molecules confined in the narrow gap, emphasizing strongly enhanced fields in these tiny spaces. Another scheme, labeled SHINERS, shows a metal nanoparticle coated by a thin dielectric shell, suggesting enhancement from the metal core while the shell isolates it from direct contact with the environment or sample.\n\nThe final method, TERS, replaces a particle assembly with a sharp scanning probe microscope tip positioned over a molecule-covered surface, concentrating the electromagnetic field at the tip apex. Overall, the figure highlights that Raman enhancement depends strongly on nanoscale geometry, especially particle junctions, gaps, and sharp tips that create localized “hot spots.”",
